WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.302 --> 00:00:07.534
Hi, welcome back to Nagarjuna.

00:00:07.534 --> 00:00:09.667
We're now starting the 5th class.

00:00:11.662 --> 00:00:13.383
We're going to start this class

00:00:13.383 --> 00:00:16.150
from the Sanskrit as we've been doing.

00:00:18.596 --> 00:00:20.987
They say, by tradition,

00:00:20.987 --> 00:00:23.436
that when you become an enlightened being,

00:00:23.436 --> 00:00:25.177
you always speak Sanskrit,

00:00:26.044 --> 00:00:29.352
and other people hear their own language.

00:00:30.314 --> 00:00:33.358
sarva bhuta ruta jnyanam, Yoga Sutra.

00:00:34.558 --> 00:00:35.861
People hear their own language.

00:00:35.861 --> 00:00:38.531
This also happened with Jesus, I think,

00:00:38.531 --> 00:00:41.719
where people would hear their
own language when he spoke.

00:00:43.228 --> 00:00:48.228
So it's traditional to cover some Sanskrit

00:00:48.430 --> 00:00:49.981
just to put a seed in your mind.

00:00:50.881 --> 00:00:55.602
So this whole class is going
to be about the question of Tim

00:00:55.602 --> 00:01:00.044
and Tim's, apostrophe S, me and mine.

00:01:01.803 --> 00:01:03.146
I don't know, when I was growing up,

00:01:03.146 --> 00:01:05.720
there was this song "I, Me, Mine."

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:09.336
And it's kind of important for this class.

00:01:09.336 --> 00:01:11.336
The idea of me and mine.

00:01:12.216 --> 00:01:15.576
Misunderstanding who is Michael

00:01:15.576 --> 00:01:20.176
and what belongs to Michael
is the source of all trouble.

00:01:21.116 --> 00:01:25.283
In the end, it's the source
of international competition,

00:01:25.283 --> 00:01:27.991
corporate competition, war,

00:01:29.958 --> 00:01:32.092
personal conflict.

00:01:32.092 --> 00:01:36.572
It all comes from a
misunderstanding of who is me

00:01:36.572 --> 00:01:39.299
and what is mine, what belongs to me.

00:01:40.316 --> 00:01:44.158
And we're all deeply invested in me

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:49.980
even at the cost of ignoring others.

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:51.468
And we all do that.

00:01:51.468 --> 00:01:55.132
I don't think anyone
can claim to really care

00:01:55.132 --> 00:01:58.892
more about someone else
than themselves in the end.

00:01:58.892 --> 00:02:02.042
So, we are by nature selfish creatures.

00:02:03.532 --> 00:02:05.718
And that's not exactly the problem.

00:02:05.718 --> 00:02:09.106
But that's a manifestation
of a deeper problem,

00:02:09.106 --> 00:02:12.645
that we don't understand who is Michael,

00:02:12.645 --> 00:02:14.300
and what belongs to Michael.

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:16.677
And if we could fix that,

00:02:16.677 --> 00:02:19.676
then the selfishness would
be fixed automatically.

00:02:19.676 --> 00:02:21.356
And then there would
be peace in the world.

00:02:21.356 --> 00:02:24.806
So, Nagarjuna, Arya Nagarjuna

00:02:24.806 --> 00:02:29.637
wants to start with the
question of me and mine.

00:02:29.637 --> 00:02:33.531
And again 'arya' itself
is a Sanskrit word.

00:02:33.531 --> 00:02:37.822
It was misused by the Nazis to mean

00:02:37.822 --> 00:02:39.797
superior race or something like that.

00:02:41.050 --> 00:02:42.835
It's an old Sanskrit word

00:02:43.692 --> 00:02:47.035
which means specifically someone

00:02:47.035 --> 00:02:51.293
who has perceived emptiness
directly, which is very rare.

00:02:52.209 --> 00:02:54.423
Maybe once every 500 years or something.

00:02:54.423 --> 00:02:58.965
So, Arya Nagarjuna, sometimes
he's called just the Arya,

00:02:59.857 --> 00:03:02.690
because I would say he's the most

00:03:03.914 --> 00:03:06.513
famous arya of all time, in our world,

00:03:06.513 --> 00:03:10.306
in our history, in the
last couple thousand years.

00:03:10.306 --> 00:03:14.305
He's the one who stands out
as an extraordinary arya.

00:03:14.305 --> 00:03:18.239
So, here's some words
that he's going to use

00:03:19.665 --> 00:03:23.764
a lot in this class. And
I thought, it would be

00:03:23.764 --> 00:03:25.994
good luck for us to go
through the Sanskrit.

00:03:25.994 --> 00:03:28.058
When you go through Sanskrit,

00:03:28.058 --> 00:03:30.667
it's called a generative language

00:03:30.667 --> 00:03:34.178
which means it acts
according to certain rules.

00:03:34.178 --> 00:03:38.155
There are 800 roots.
If you know the roots,

00:03:38.155 --> 00:03:42.037
and you know the rules,
you can speak Sanskrit

00:03:42.037 --> 00:03:45.386
and write Sanskrit without
ever seeing Sanskrit

00:03:45.386 --> 00:03:47.868
because the rules are perfect.

00:03:47.868 --> 00:03:50.884
It's a perfect, Sanskrit
means 'the perfect language.'

00:03:50.884 --> 00:03:53.979
Sanskrta means 'the perfect language.'

00:03:53.979 --> 00:03:54.812
And it means

00:03:54.812 --> 00:03:59.803
you could predict the whole language

00:04:00.748 --> 00:04:04.429
just from the 800 roots
because it's perfect.

00:04:04.429 --> 00:04:06.556
All forms are perfect.

00:04:06.556 --> 00:04:08.256
So we're going to go through...

00:04:08.256 --> 00:04:10.613
When you discuss Sanskrit words,

00:04:10.613 --> 00:04:12.453
it's fun to look at the root.

00:04:13.587 --> 00:04:15.942
It's fun to see how it's generated,

00:04:15.942 --> 00:04:17.845
how the word is generated.

00:04:17.845 --> 00:04:21.756
And then all Western languages,

00:04:21.756 --> 00:04:26.405
from Iceland to Iran, come from Sanskrit.

00:04:26.405 --> 00:04:31.405
So, kick out the Semetic
languages, like Hebrew or Arabic.

00:04:31.996 --> 00:04:34.258
Kick out Chinese, and I don't know,

00:04:34.258 --> 00:04:36.804
Alaskan, Navajo, or something.

00:04:36.804 --> 00:04:38.722
The rest of the world's languages

00:04:38.722 --> 00:04:40.420
come directly from Sanskrit.

00:04:40.420 --> 00:04:43.363
And Latin and Greek were
derived from Sanskrit.

00:04:44.205 --> 00:04:46.359
English is basically
derived from Sanskrit.

00:04:46.359 --> 00:04:51.158
So when you want to
understand a Sanskrit word,

00:04:51.158 --> 00:04:53.701
it's fun to look at the cognate.

00:04:53.701 --> 00:04:56.160
Cognate means give me some English words

00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:58.837
that come from this root.

00:04:58.837 --> 00:05:00.542
And I always tell people,

00:05:00.542 --> 00:05:02.476
if you really were into it,

00:05:02.476 --> 00:05:04.762
you can pick up the New York Times,

00:05:04.762 --> 00:05:09.356
and see the Sanskrit, which
is kind of a fun exercise.

00:05:09.356 --> 00:05:13.030
You can see the whole page of
Sanskrit that it came from.

00:05:13.030 --> 00:05:14.565
So, let's try that.

00:05:14.565 --> 00:05:17.814
The first word I'd like
to cover is 'aham',

00:05:17.814 --> 00:05:21.156
which is a-h-a-m.

00:05:21.156 --> 00:05:22.765
And it's the Sanskrit word

00:05:22.765 --> 00:05:26.815
for 'me', 'I'. And the Tibetan is 'nga.'

00:05:27.768 --> 00:05:30.098
By the way, Tibetan is
totally off the charts.

00:05:30.098 --> 00:05:34.248
No linguistic connection to
any language in the world.

00:05:34.248 --> 00:05:39.248
There's a tradition, that
it came from outer space

00:05:40.119 --> 00:05:42.006
or higher beings.
[GMR laughing]

00:05:42.006 --> 00:05:45.670
Linguistically, it's believable

00:05:45.670 --> 00:05:47.111
because there's not a
single word connected

00:05:47.111 --> 00:05:48.271
to any other language.

00:05:48.271 --> 00:05:51.700
But anyways, aham means me.

00:05:52.672 --> 00:05:53.994
And it's the root of the word 'ego'.

00:05:53.994 --> 00:05:56.930
H and Gs are pretty much interchangeable,

00:05:57.997 --> 00:06:01.287
and As normally become E in English.

00:06:01.287 --> 00:06:04.747
So the word 'ego' comes from 'aham.'

00:06:04.747 --> 00:06:07.408
So that means 'me' in Sanskrit.

00:06:07.408 --> 00:06:10.197
Probably 'me' comes from 'aham'.

00:06:10.197 --> 00:06:15.197
And then 'nga yi ba' in Tibetan,
means 'mama' in Sanskrit,

00:06:15.534 --> 00:06:17.892
and 'mama' means mine.

00:06:17.892 --> 00:06:21.059
So aham means 'me', and mama means 'mine'.

00:06:21.951 --> 00:06:24.724
And that's going to be important for us.

00:06:24.724 --> 00:06:26.982
You can obviously see how the word 'me'

00:06:26.982 --> 00:06:30.157
and the word 'mine' are cognates that

00:06:30.157 --> 00:06:33.081
come from the word 'mama.'

00:06:33.081 --> 00:06:36.524
Then there is an important new word

00:06:36.524 --> 00:06:40.399
made from both of these two
words with the root √kr̩.

00:06:41.339 --> 00:06:45.829
And the root √kr̩ which
is k-i-r in pronunciation

00:06:45.829 --> 00:06:48.102
means to make something.

00:06:48.102 --> 00:06:51.260
And we have the word
'karma', comes from that,

00:06:51.260 --> 00:06:53.229
because karma makes the world.

00:06:53.229 --> 00:06:56.484
English cognates for √kr̩ are 'cereal,'

00:06:57.833 --> 00:07:00.584
which is the grain, which
is made by the earth.

00:07:02.685 --> 00:07:04.293
The C used to be K,

00:07:05.177 --> 00:07:08.896
and then K changed to an S sound.

00:07:08.896 --> 00:07:13.311
After... Just before
Shakespeare or something.

00:07:13.311 --> 00:07:15.782
A lot of Cs became, like 'city,'

00:07:15.782 --> 00:07:18.756
changed from K sound to S sound.

00:07:18.756 --> 00:07:23.322
So √kr̩ and 'create' comes from that,

00:07:23.322 --> 00:07:24.580
with the same C.

00:07:27.478 --> 00:07:31.605
So, I want to talk about
ahamkāra and mamakāra.

00:07:31.605 --> 00:07:34.038
Ahaṃkāra and mamakāra.

00:07:34.972 --> 00:07:39.780
So if you connect 'me' to 'kāra,'

00:07:40.773 --> 00:07:42.135
it's 'making me.'

00:07:42.135 --> 00:07:45.216
You're running around making me.

00:07:45.216 --> 00:07:47.670
And 'mamakāra' means you're running around

00:07:47.670 --> 00:07:51.062
making my, mine.

00:07:51.062 --> 00:07:56.062
So 'ahaṃkāra' means 'making me',

00:07:56.432 --> 00:07:59.565
and 'mamakāra' means 'making mine'.

00:08:00.533 --> 00:08:03.115
And that's an important
idea to think about.

00:08:03.115 --> 00:08:05.273
That you are running around the world,

00:08:05.273 --> 00:08:08.769
you are defining me, and
you are defining mine.

00:08:10.177 --> 00:08:13.199
And the way you do it so sloppily

00:08:13.199 --> 00:08:15.657
causes a lot of conflicts in the world.

00:08:15.657 --> 00:08:19.019
People start disagreeing
about whose is mine

00:08:19.019 --> 00:08:21.315
and which is not mine.

00:08:21.315 --> 00:08:22.148
All right?

00:08:23.883 --> 00:08:28.883
The 'm' in 'aham' changes to n before k.

00:08:29.214 --> 00:08:32.285
So you wouldn't say 'ahaṃkāra,'

00:08:32.285 --> 00:08:35.043
you would say '[ahang kara]. Try it.

00:08:35.043 --> 00:08:38.028
Ahaṃkāra [ahang kara],
ahaṃkāra [ahang kara].

00:08:38.028 --> 00:08:40.945
And that long ā is ah instead of a.

00:08:42.124 --> 00:08:46.541
So ahaṃ kāra, ahaṃ kāra, say, ahaṃ kāra

00:08:48.662 --> 00:08:51.595
Then 'mamakāra' is easy, mamakāra.

00:08:51.595 --> 00:08:52.428
All right,

00:08:54.328 --> 00:08:56.050
the real, correct translation

00:08:56.050 --> 00:09:00.825
is NGAR 'DZIN,

00:09:00.825 --> 00:09:02.804
and NGA YIR 'DZIN

00:09:02.804 --> 00:09:06.684
NGAR 'DZIN, which means to grab on to me,

00:09:07.726 --> 00:09:11.862
and then NGA YI BA 'DZIN,
which means to grab on to mine.

00:09:11.862 --> 00:09:16.807
So these are two emotions
that we all have,

00:09:16.807 --> 00:09:20.877
that are the root of
trouble in this world.

00:09:20.877 --> 00:09:25.184
If people could remove this ahaṃkāra

00:09:25.184 --> 00:09:28.092
and this mamakāra,

00:09:28.092 --> 00:09:31.100
then the world would be a beautiful place.

00:09:31.100 --> 00:09:36.059
What do you call a beautiful
place, world in Sanskrit?

00:09:36.893 --> 00:09:38.686
Mandala.

00:09:38.686 --> 00:09:43.476
And the Spanish word 'mundo'
comes from the same root,

00:09:43.476 --> 00:09:44.653
means the world.

00:09:44.653 --> 00:09:48.727
It actually means a 'disc.'
That's a long story, but...

00:09:48.727 --> 00:09:53.594
A perfect world, a
perfect mundo is mandala.

00:09:59.356 --> 00:10:04.348
There are some... I
enjoy going to churches,

00:10:05.574 --> 00:10:08.102
I enjoy going to ashrams.

00:10:08.102 --> 00:10:12.477
I spent 10 years going to one ashram

00:10:13.644 --> 00:10:16.040
where they teach Hindu philosophy.

00:10:16.907 --> 00:10:19.273
And I used to go there and listen,

00:10:20.830 --> 00:10:23.820
and I recall that they had an ahaṃkāra

00:10:23.820 --> 00:10:25.142
in the Sankya school.

00:10:25.142 --> 00:10:28.885
So this is one of the most
important schools of Hinduism.

00:10:28.885 --> 00:10:30.489
Probably the main school of

00:10:30.489 --> 00:10:34.972
the ancient traditional
yoga schools is Sankya.

00:10:34.972 --> 00:10:39.347
They have this idea
about ahaṃkāra, which...

00:10:39.347 --> 00:10:43.738
You see, Buddhism was prevalent

00:10:43.738 --> 00:10:47.263
in India for a 1,000 years,
and then it died out.

00:10:48.554 --> 00:10:51.877
It waned, and then it came back again.

00:10:51.877 --> 00:10:56.652
And when Buddhism was collapsing,

00:10:58.330 --> 00:11:02.282
a lot of the words got
borrowed by other traditions.

00:11:02.282 --> 00:11:05.401
And then they took on some
really weird meanings.

00:11:05.401 --> 00:11:08.209
So, this ahaṃkāra is a good example.

00:11:09.829 --> 00:11:13.277
Grabbing on to me and grabbing on to mine.

00:11:13.277 --> 00:11:16.586
I'll give you the
definition from the internet

00:11:16.586 --> 00:11:19.669
which is always a trustworthy source.

00:11:20.986 --> 00:11:25.986
The ahaṃkāra are a mysterious
and powerful species

00:11:26.245 --> 00:11:28.838
of shape shifting, wish granting creatures

00:11:28.838 --> 00:11:31.183
that first appeared in the solar system

00:11:31.183 --> 00:11:33.101
after the traveler arrived.

00:11:33.101 --> 00:11:38.101
So, these very basic, important,

00:11:38.353 --> 00:11:43.344
beautiful, precious ideas about who am I,

00:11:48.234 --> 00:11:51.554
they got totally weirded out in India.

00:11:51.554 --> 00:11:55.684
So why I'm saying that is not
to criticize other traditions,

00:11:55.684 --> 00:11:58.811
but to encourage you to be careful.

00:11:58.811 --> 00:12:01.905
When you are attracted to a tradition,

00:12:01.905 --> 00:12:04.578
then try to dig into
it, try to look at it,

00:12:04.578 --> 00:12:09.578
try to learn it carefully,
try to see if it's authentic.

00:12:10.408 --> 00:12:13.036
And the tradition that
we're teaching today

00:12:13.036 --> 00:12:14.522
is 2,000 years old.

00:12:14.522 --> 00:12:18.300
It hasn't really changed
in 2,000 years at all.

00:12:18.300 --> 00:12:20.764
Which is why I can sit
and teach you a text

00:12:20.764 --> 00:12:22.497
which is 2,000 years old.

00:12:22.497 --> 00:12:24.756
The language is the same.

00:12:24.756 --> 00:12:25.873
You ready?

00:12:25.873 --> 00:12:27.548
Whan that Aprille with his shoures soote,

00:12:27.548 --> 00:12:30.014
The droghte of March
hath perced to the roote,

00:12:30.014 --> 00:12:32.964
And bathed every veyne in swich licóur

00:12:35.578 --> 00:12:37.436
English, 500 years ago.

00:12:38.528 --> 00:12:39.887
Chaucer.

00:12:39.887 --> 00:12:44.887
So take Nagarjuna's 2,000
years old, four times older,

00:12:45.376 --> 00:12:47.474
but you can easily read it.

00:12:47.474 --> 00:12:49.175
It's a very strict tradition.

00:12:49.175 --> 00:12:50.939
It's kept very tight.

00:12:50.939 --> 00:12:55.557
So I encourage you not
to spend your precious,

00:12:55.557 --> 00:12:59.169
irreplaceable life hours without checking

00:12:59.169 --> 00:13:01.494
if a tradition is authentic.

00:13:01.494 --> 00:13:03.153
Otherwise, you might run into

00:13:03.153 --> 00:13:05.553
solar system traveler or something.

00:13:07.210 --> 00:13:08.043
All right.

00:13:09.363 --> 00:13:10.750
We've reached an interesting point.

00:13:10.750 --> 00:13:13.591
He says, look, we discussed Tim,

00:13:14.603 --> 00:13:16.520
and we discussed Tim's.

00:13:18.128 --> 00:13:19.763
But how do you use that in your real life?

00:13:19.763 --> 00:13:21.582
Come on, let's get real.

00:13:21.582 --> 00:13:25.444
And Nagarjuna is sensitive
enough to the student,

00:13:25.444 --> 00:13:30.051
that he doesn't just keep
pouring philosophy on your head.

00:13:30.051 --> 00:13:33.021
He'll say, now he pauses and he says,

00:13:33.021 --> 00:13:35.111
"How are we going to use that?"

00:13:35.111 --> 00:13:37.651
How is that supposed to improve your life

00:13:37.651 --> 00:13:40.280
for you to understand about me and mine?

00:13:40.280 --> 00:13:42.508
How's that going to improve your life?

00:13:42.508 --> 00:13:47.400
And then we have the next section here.

00:13:47.400 --> 00:13:48.565
It starts here.

00:13:48.565 --> 00:13:50.249
,BDAG DANG BDAG GI ZHI BA'I PHYIR,

00:13:50.249 --> 00:13:52.174
,NGAR 'DZIN NGA YIR 'DZIN MED 'GYUR,

00:13:52.174 --> 00:13:53.790
,NGA DANG NGA YIR 'DZIN MED GANG,

00:13:53.790 --> 00:13:55.165
,DE YANG YOD PA MA YIN TE,

00:13:55.165 --> 00:13:56.931
,NGAR 'DZIN NGAR YIR 'DZIN MED PAR,

00:13:56.931 --> 00:13:59.673
,GANG GIS MTHONG BAS MI MTHONG NGO,

00:14:01.219 --> 00:14:03.332
I'm going to read these
two paragraphs verbatim,

00:14:03.332 --> 00:14:06.332
because the two paragraphs
have explanation.

00:14:06.332 --> 00:14:07.931
Because I think they're very beautiful.

00:14:08.856 --> 00:14:10.330
NGA DANG NGAR 'DZIN GYI YUL

00:14:10.330 --> 00:14:11.838
BDAG DANG BDAG GI BA'I MTSAN MA

00:14:11.838 --> 00:14:12.963
NYE BAR ZHI BA'I PHYIR

00:14:16.208 --> 00:14:18.047
Your idea of who you are,

00:14:18.047 --> 00:14:21.739
and your idea of what
belongs to you will melt

00:14:21.739 --> 00:14:25.402
when you truly understand
the contradictions

00:14:25.402 --> 00:14:27.470
of how you think about yourself.

00:14:27.470 --> 00:14:28.994
ZHI BA'I PHYIR RNAL 'BYOR PAS

00:14:28.994 --> 00:14:31.312
NGA RANG BZHIN GYIS GRUB PAR 'DZIN PA DANG

00:14:32.316 --> 00:14:34.299
So, what does a RNAL 'BYOR PA do?

00:14:34.299 --> 00:14:36.832
What's the Sanskrit, Tim? RNAL 'BYOR

00:14:37.890 --> 00:14:39.293
Yogi.

00:14:39.293 --> 00:14:41.126
So really in the old days,

00:14:41.126 --> 00:14:44.613
yogi meant a person
who understood reality.

00:14:44.613 --> 00:14:47.161
And in Tibetan, the word is

00:14:48.003 --> 00:14:50.713
'the person who applies
their meditation perfectly',

00:14:50.713 --> 00:14:53.579
it's the translation of the Sanskrit yogi.

00:14:55.323 --> 00:14:58.856
So, how does a great meditator

00:14:59.690 --> 00:15:01.782
view me and mine?

00:15:01.782 --> 00:15:03.414
NGA RANG BZHIN GYIS GRUB PAR 'DZIN PA DANG

00:15:03.414 --> 00:15:04.581
NGA YI BA RANG BZHIN GYIS GRUB PAR

00:15:04.581 --> 00:15:06.565
'DZIN PA'I 'JIG LTA SPANGS

00:15:06.565 --> 00:15:09.467
They have eliminated a misunderstanding

00:15:10.475 --> 00:15:13.589
of who is me and what is mine.

00:15:13.589 --> 00:15:16.876
I think it's easier if
you start with mine.

00:15:16.876 --> 00:15:18.722
Things that are mine.

00:15:18.722 --> 00:15:21.297
And I don't know.

00:15:23.600 --> 00:15:25.298
When you pick up this green bottle

00:15:25.298 --> 00:15:27.016
that we talked about before,

00:15:29.092 --> 00:15:33.400
we instinctively feel
with our deepest instincts

00:15:34.380 --> 00:15:37.010
that it's coming from itself.

00:15:37.010 --> 00:15:41.575
That the bottle is
presenting itself to me,

00:15:41.575 --> 00:15:43.113
and it's appearing to me.

00:15:43.113 --> 00:15:46.513
And no matter how many
times you talk about it,

00:15:48.854 --> 00:15:53.854
it's an almost unstoppable
habit of human beings

00:15:54.157 --> 00:15:57.871
and all sentient creatures to believe

00:15:57.871 --> 00:16:00.092
that things are coming at them.

00:16:00.092 --> 00:16:02.797
Like if I hold up this green bottle,

00:16:02.797 --> 00:16:05.894
you wouldn't say, "Oh dang, I
should've made a red bottle."

00:16:05.894 --> 00:16:09.175
You don't assume that the
bottle is coming from you.

00:16:09.175 --> 00:16:12.246
You think, "Oh this
bottle came on a truck,

00:16:12.246 --> 00:16:13.693
"it was made in a factory.

00:16:13.693 --> 00:16:15.821
They got the water, they poured it in."

00:16:15.821 --> 00:16:18.097
But if you really understand Nagarjuna,

00:16:19.672 --> 00:16:21.691
the bottle did come from you,

00:16:21.691 --> 00:16:23.417
and the bottle is coming from you,

00:16:23.417 --> 00:16:25.507
and the green color is coming from you.

00:16:25.507 --> 00:16:27.405
All of these are... And by the way,

00:16:27.405 --> 00:16:29.667
if your meditation gets really good,

00:16:29.667 --> 00:16:31.875
you can catch yourself making it green.

00:16:33.125 --> 00:16:35.929
If your meditation gets
really, really good,

00:16:35.929 --> 00:16:37.704
you can watch yourself,

00:16:37.704 --> 00:16:39.835
your mind painting it green.

00:16:39.835 --> 00:16:42.282
Then you're like, "Oh my gosh,

00:16:42.282 --> 00:16:47.282
then all the troubles in my
life are coming from me."

00:16:47.501 --> 00:16:50.282
And then you become a
much more pleasant person,

00:16:50.282 --> 00:16:52.615
because you don't blame
anybody else for your problems.

00:16:52.615 --> 00:16:55.421
It doesn't mean your problems go away,

00:16:55.421 --> 00:16:58.988
but it means that whole persona

00:16:59.870 --> 00:17:02.684
of a person who blames
the world for things,

00:17:02.684 --> 00:17:06.235
even bad drivers, stuff
like that, disappears.

00:17:06.235 --> 00:17:08.525
You don't blame other people for stuff,

00:17:08.525 --> 00:17:10.279
because you know it's coming from you.

00:17:10.279 --> 00:17:14.351
And just imagine if you got
that part of your mind back,

00:17:14.351 --> 00:17:16.081
if you got that much of your mind back,

00:17:16.081 --> 00:17:17.696
the part of your mind which is blaming

00:17:17.696 --> 00:17:20.696
other people and things for life.

00:17:20.696 --> 00:17:24.376
If you got that time back,
how would you change?

00:17:24.376 --> 00:17:27.383
And how would your attitudes
towards people change?

00:17:27.383 --> 00:17:30.245
How often could you get upset

00:17:30.245 --> 00:17:34.942
if you knew, all the crap
in the world was exact,

00:17:34.942 --> 00:17:38.147
was precisely produced by your own mind?

00:17:38.147 --> 00:17:40.080
Then you would just fix your mind.

00:17:40.080 --> 00:17:42.354
You would focus on that, and already

00:17:42.354 --> 00:17:45.737
you'd be incredibly happier person.

00:17:45.737 --> 00:17:48.443
You'd be a much more pleasant person.

00:17:48.443 --> 00:17:49.793
So anyway,

00:17:49.793 --> 00:17:51.798
RANG BZHIN GYIS GRUB PAR 'DZIN PA'I

00:17:51.798 --> 00:17:53.600
So he's talking about a yogi

00:17:53.600 --> 00:17:55.226
who has overcome the feeling that things

00:17:55.226 --> 00:17:56.890
are not coming from them

00:17:56.890 --> 00:17:59.295
and they understand that
things are coming from them.

00:18:00.453 --> 00:18:03.520
For example, Tim is coming from Tim.

00:18:04.773 --> 00:18:08.339
And Tim was not a name put by
his mom on his body and mind.

00:18:08.339 --> 00:18:11.478
His body, the way his body acts,

00:18:11.478 --> 00:18:13.627
the way his mind acts,

00:18:13.627 --> 00:18:16.072
are coming from seeds in his own mind.

00:18:16.072 --> 00:18:20.445
And he could adjust his
body and mind infinitely.

00:18:20.445 --> 00:18:22.307
If that's true.

00:18:22.307 --> 00:18:23.948
NGA YI BA RANG BZHIN
GYIS GRUB PAR 'DZIN PA'I

00:18:23.948 --> 00:18:25.600
'JIG LTA SPANGS NAS

00:18:25.600 --> 00:18:30.059
So let's suppose you
have overcome the habit,

00:18:30.059 --> 00:18:34.757
the natural instinct that
things are coming this way.

00:18:34.757 --> 00:18:35.985
And suddenly you understand that

00:18:35.985 --> 00:18:38.417
they're flowing out of your mind.

00:18:38.417 --> 00:18:39.382
You are making pictures,

00:18:39.382 --> 00:18:42.043
and then you're giving them names.

00:18:42.043 --> 00:18:44.459
By the way, it doesn't
mean they're not real.

00:18:44.459 --> 00:18:47.521
I used to teach this in
New York. She was there.

00:18:47.521 --> 00:18:49.946
I'd say to a student... They'd
say, "Oh, nothing's real."

00:18:49.946 --> 00:18:51.850
I'd say, "Go out, and
stand in front of a taxi

00:18:51.850 --> 00:18:53.981
on 5th avenue. Tell me what happens."

00:18:53.981 --> 00:18:55.771
You'd break your leg,

00:18:55.771 --> 00:18:58.474
and you'd get a not real hospital bill.

00:18:58.474 --> 00:19:00.635
And you'd stay on a not real hospital bed.

00:19:00.635 --> 00:19:03.906
They'd put a not real
cast on your not real leg.

00:19:04.956 --> 00:19:06.835
It's real, but it's coming from you.

00:19:08.227 --> 00:19:10.038
Which is cool because it's real,

00:19:10.038 --> 00:19:13.571
but it's adjustable by personal effort.

00:19:13.571 --> 00:19:14.404
All right.

00:19:14.404 --> 00:19:16.712
'JIG LTA GNYIS KYI ZHEN
YUL MED PAR RTOGS PA'I

00:19:16.712 --> 00:19:19.221
YANG DAG PA'I DON LA ZHUGS PA'I PHYIR

00:19:21.506 --> 00:19:25.058
So, they have overcome their habit

00:19:25.058 --> 00:19:27.655
of misunderstanding themselves,

00:19:27.655 --> 00:19:30.075
where they come from, and
where the world comes from.

00:19:32.600 --> 00:19:34.125
You got this picture here?

00:19:35.349 --> 00:19:38.544
I don't know, it's a girl
with a book, I think,

00:19:38.544 --> 00:19:40.596
a young lady with a book.

00:19:40.596 --> 00:19:42.478
Yes.

00:19:42.478 --> 00:19:44.371
I saw this pictures I said
I've got to use this picture.

00:19:45.280 --> 00:19:47.246
She's like meditating.

00:19:47.246 --> 00:19:50.726
And what you're supposed to meditate on,

00:19:50.726 --> 00:19:53.748
when you try to meditate on emptiness,

00:19:53.748 --> 00:19:56.252
is to try to understand the emptiness

00:19:56.252 --> 00:19:59.469
not of unrelated things,

00:19:59.469 --> 00:20:01.044
but the emptiness of you,

00:20:02.034 --> 00:20:03.522
and the emptiness of mine.

00:20:03.522 --> 00:20:05.923
The things that are your
personal possessions.

00:20:05.923 --> 00:20:08.018
Things that you own.

00:20:08.018 --> 00:20:09.926
So, I thought it's a perfect picture.

00:20:09.926 --> 00:20:13.740
Because you can imagine, she's
meditating on who is she,

00:20:13.740 --> 00:20:15.395
where did she come from?

00:20:15.395 --> 00:20:18.499
And she's meditating on where
her possessions came from.

00:20:18.499 --> 00:20:21.580
And the book... I imagine
the book is by Nagarjuna,

00:20:21.580 --> 00:20:24.459
who's telling her where
the book came from.

00:20:24.459 --> 00:20:25.292
Anyway.

00:20:25.292 --> 00:20:26.725
DE DAG DANG DE LAS
GZHAN PA'I NGAR 'DZIN PA

00:20:26.725 --> 00:20:29.099
DANG NGA YIR 'DZIN PA MED CING SPONG BA

00:20:29.099 --> 00:20:31.241
GANG YIN PA'I RNAL 'BYOR
PA DE YANG NGO BO NYID

00:20:31.241 --> 00:20:33.102
KYIS YOD PA MA YIN TE,

00:20:33.102 --> 00:20:35.002
But then he says,

00:20:35.002 --> 00:20:36.507
"Let's consider the person

00:20:36.507 --> 00:20:39.652
who understands me and mine."

00:20:39.652 --> 00:20:42.818
Now don't focus on how
they understand themselves,

00:20:43.652 --> 00:20:45.080
and don't focus on how they understand

00:20:45.080 --> 00:20:46.680
the things that they own.

00:20:46.680 --> 00:20:49.310
Focus on the person who understands, okay?

00:20:49.310 --> 00:20:51.314
Lets see if we can understand the person

00:20:51.314 --> 00:20:54.447
who understands themselves
and what they own.

00:20:55.455 --> 00:20:57.688
GANG GIS NGAR 'DZIN PA
DANG NGA YIR 'DZIN PA

00:20:57.688 --> 00:20:59.361
MED PAR SPONG BA'I RNAL 'BYOR PA

00:20:59.361 --> 00:21:01.961
NGO BOS YOD DO SNYAM DU MTHONG BA STE

00:21:03.186 --> 00:21:06.995
If you believe that that
person comes from themselves,

00:21:08.245 --> 00:21:11.106
if you believe that that
person is coming from them,

00:21:11.106 --> 00:21:12.908
so you are observing a
person who understands

00:21:12.908 --> 00:21:16.536
who they are and where
their stuff is coming from.

00:21:16.536 --> 00:21:19.166
But now you're observing that person,

00:21:19.166 --> 00:21:22.094
and if you think NGO BOS YOD DO SNYAM DU,

00:21:22.094 --> 00:21:25.076
if you think they are
coming from their selves,

00:21:25.076 --> 00:21:27.566
if you look at them and think

00:21:27.566 --> 00:21:29.655
that the yogi is coming from themselves,

00:21:29.655 --> 00:21:32.569
if you look at the person
who understands me and mine,

00:21:32.569 --> 00:21:33.740
and you get the feeling that

00:21:33.740 --> 00:21:36.840
they must be coming then from themselves.

00:21:36.840 --> 00:21:38.692
If you look at it that way, he says,

00:21:38.692 --> 00:21:40.592
if you see it that way - MTHONG STE

00:21:40.592 --> 00:21:41.703
MTHONG, like MTHONG LAM,

00:21:41.703 --> 00:21:42.536
right JB?

00:21:43.578 --> 00:21:46.264
DE KHO NA NYID KYI DON MI MTHONG

00:21:46.264 --> 00:21:48.378
Then we can say you don't see anything.

00:21:48.378 --> 00:21:50.568
It's very cute.

00:21:50.568 --> 00:21:52.201
If you think you could lay your eyes

00:21:52.201 --> 00:21:54.309
on a person who
understands all this stuff,

00:21:54.309 --> 00:21:55.972
you don't see nothing.

00:21:57.122 --> 00:22:00.490
Then Nagarjuna's really rubbing it in.

00:22:00.490 --> 00:22:01.323
- Hey, did you ever meet a person

00:22:01.323 --> 00:22:02.894
who understood me and mine?

00:22:02.894 --> 00:22:05.877
- Oh yes. - Yes? Did you look at them?

00:22:05.877 --> 00:22:08.277
- Yes. - Yes? Well, you didn't.

00:22:09.661 --> 00:22:13.216
He's like that. He gets very frustrating.

00:22:14.200 --> 00:22:15.290
And this gets into...

00:22:15.290 --> 00:22:16.641
By the way, we're going to go to there,

00:22:16.641 --> 00:22:19.830
we're going to go directly
to the Diamond Cutter Sutra.

00:22:19.830 --> 00:22:22.449
The emptiness of the Diamond Cutter Sutra.

00:22:22.449 --> 00:22:24.857
And I like to tell my students in Asia

00:22:24.857 --> 00:22:28.473
who are very devoted to the
Diamond Cutter Sutra as I am,

00:22:30.808 --> 00:22:32.083
"Who wrote it?

00:22:32.958 --> 00:22:34.584
You did."

00:22:34.584 --> 00:22:36.599
And when you read the Bible, or you see

00:22:36.599 --> 00:22:38.948
"Gone With The Wind,"

00:22:40.249 --> 00:22:41.575
according to these ideas,

00:22:41.575 --> 00:22:44.270
you made them. You made them.

00:22:44.270 --> 00:22:46.178
You made "Gone With The Wind."

00:22:46.178 --> 00:22:47.586
You're the director.

00:22:48.439 --> 00:22:51.283
Every time you turn a page in the gospel,

00:22:52.158 --> 00:22:55.139
your mind is laying down
the words one by one.

00:22:55.139 --> 00:22:58.262
And it's so quick that
you don't notice it.

00:22:58.262 --> 00:23:00.375
And you don't see it.

00:23:00.375 --> 00:23:03.534
But you are actually... you are the author

00:23:03.534 --> 00:23:05.209
of your own salvation.

00:23:06.967 --> 00:23:09.460
You are the one who's
writing the religious book

00:23:09.460 --> 00:23:10.845
that finally calls to you

00:23:10.845 --> 00:23:13.878
and pulls you out of this problem.

00:23:13.878 --> 00:23:15.047
And it's a cool idea.

00:23:15.047 --> 00:23:17.670
God comes from you. Jesus comes from you.

00:23:17.670 --> 00:23:19.926
Buddha comes from you.

00:23:19.926 --> 00:23:22.476
How can I create a being

00:23:22.476 --> 00:23:24.342
who knows so much more than me,

00:23:24.342 --> 00:23:26.580
that they can pull me out of trouble?

00:23:26.580 --> 00:23:30.606
That's the cool thing
because karma escalates.

00:23:30.606 --> 00:23:33.575
Karma tends to multiply on its own.

00:23:33.575 --> 00:23:35.358
A small understanding can become a...

00:23:36.225 --> 00:23:38.327
If it's well-handled,

00:23:38.327 --> 00:23:41.860
a small understanding can
become a blanket understanding

00:23:41.860 --> 00:23:44.222
of the whole universe.

00:23:44.222 --> 00:23:47.283
And these diving things, angels, God,

00:23:47.283 --> 00:23:50.534
Jesus, Mohammed, Buddha,
they're all coming from you.

00:23:50.534 --> 00:23:53.916
If you live in a world
where these people walked

00:23:53.916 --> 00:23:56.024
you're the one who brought them here.

00:23:56.024 --> 00:23:57.854
And that's cool. And if you think not,

00:23:57.854 --> 00:24:00.003
then you don't understand
what they were talking about.

00:24:01.228 --> 00:24:03.206
That's beautiful too, right?

00:24:03.206 --> 00:24:06.258
So anyway, that's the point
that Nagarjuna's making.

00:24:07.299 --> 00:24:09.247
Here's the boy.

00:24:09.247 --> 00:24:10.854
I put "Those who see, do not."

00:24:10.854 --> 00:24:12.894
That's the theme.

00:24:12.894 --> 00:24:15.849
This theme in Buddhism is
called "Those who see, do not."

00:24:15.849 --> 00:24:18.799
Those who believe that the people

00:24:18.799 --> 00:24:20.193
who came into this world to teach

00:24:20.193 --> 00:24:23.018
the deepest things - like Jesus,

00:24:23.018 --> 00:24:24.976
like Buddha, like Mohammed -

00:24:26.344 --> 00:24:30.145
if they believe those
people came into this world,

00:24:30.145 --> 00:24:31.920
and didn't come from us,

00:24:31.920 --> 00:24:34.135
then they have never met those people.

00:24:34.135 --> 00:24:36.468
It's very interesting.

00:24:37.410 --> 00:24:40.870
If you think that's where
these people came from,

00:24:40.870 --> 00:24:41.974
then you don't understand

00:24:41.974 --> 00:24:44.869
what they're trying to tell you:

00:24:44.869 --> 00:24:46.875
that you can be perfected,

00:24:46.875 --> 00:24:49.688
that you can make yourself perfected.

00:24:49.688 --> 00:24:50.521
All right.

00:24:55.846 --> 00:24:56.704
Next verse.

00:24:56.704 --> 00:24:59.061
,NANG DANG PHYI ROL NYID DAG LA,

00:24:59.061 --> 00:25:00.778
,BDAG DANG BDAG GI SNYAM ZAD NA,

00:25:00.778 --> 00:25:02.911
,NYE BAR LEN PA 'GAG 'GYUR ZHING,

00:25:02.911 --> 00:25:04.369
,DE ZAD PAS NA SKYE BA ZAD,

00:25:05.357 --> 00:25:07.542
He's starting to get into
practical applications.

00:25:08.575 --> 00:25:12.221
The important word here is NYE BAR LEN PA.

00:25:13.204 --> 00:25:15.101
Which one is it in the
Sanskrit, Christina?

00:25:16.043 --> 00:25:17.742
It means to take something.

00:25:17.742 --> 00:25:19.816
It should start with upa.

00:25:19.816 --> 00:25:24.816
[Indistinct]

00:25:27.095 --> 00:25:28.620
No, I don't think so.

00:25:30.189 --> 00:25:33.689
I think it's the upādānaṃ, yes, upādānaṃ.

00:25:34.728 --> 00:25:35.561
NYE BAR LEN PA

00:25:35.561 --> 00:25:38.977
means to take something on.

00:25:38.977 --> 00:25:42.353
And it's going to be
crucial to this verse.

00:25:42.353 --> 00:25:46.433
The most classic reference of this word is

00:25:46.433 --> 00:25:49.547
what happens to your body
and mind when you're born:

00:25:49.547 --> 00:25:51.609
you take it on.

00:25:51.609 --> 00:25:53.489
You assume a body

00:25:53.489 --> 00:25:56.117
and you assume a mind when you're born.

00:25:56.117 --> 00:26:01.117
So he says here, "You
can stop your grasping,

00:26:01.386 --> 00:26:03.884
"or you can stop your grabbing,

00:26:05.051 --> 00:26:08.112
"if you understand that me
and mine are coming from you

00:26:08.112 --> 00:26:10.496
"and not from the outside."

00:26:10.496 --> 00:26:13.093
Then you can stop grasping to things.

00:26:13.093 --> 00:26:16.336
And it's kind of obvious
in the sense of money.

00:26:17.478 --> 00:26:19.449
I don't know, my life, my relationship

00:26:19.449 --> 00:26:20.509
with money has changed.

00:26:20.509 --> 00:26:22.491
I didn't have any money when I was a kid.

00:26:22.491 --> 00:26:26.508
We were even getting
food from the food bank.

00:26:30.241 --> 00:26:32.399
And then I was brought up
in a culture that said,

00:26:32.399 --> 00:26:33.727
"Don't focus on money."

00:26:33.727 --> 00:26:37.098
I was brought up in the
God-bless hippie culture,

00:26:37.098 --> 00:26:38.523
which said you are not defined

00:26:38.523 --> 00:26:40.543
by how much money you make.

00:26:40.543 --> 00:26:43.437
So I kind of avoided
money for a long time.

00:26:43.437 --> 00:26:47.037
Then John Brady's project got in trouble.

00:26:47.037 --> 00:26:48.812
We were borrowing money all the time

00:26:48.812 --> 00:26:50.546
to save the ancient books.

00:26:50.546 --> 00:26:51.931
And I was thinking, well maybe I should

00:26:51.931 --> 00:26:56.064
use the philosophy to make some money.

00:26:56.064 --> 00:26:57.500
So I did.

00:26:57.500 --> 00:26:58.643
And the diamond company we built

00:26:58.643 --> 00:27:01.315
is now the largest in the world.

00:27:01.315 --> 00:27:04.732
And was brought by Warren Buffet in 2009.

00:27:05.931 --> 00:27:08.731
And I think it's useful to have money

00:27:09.790 --> 00:27:11.811
because you can use it.

00:27:11.811 --> 00:27:15.372
And we support hundreds of poor people,

00:27:15.372 --> 00:27:17.303
hundreds and hundreds of refugees.

00:27:17.303 --> 00:27:21.470
We have 532 kids in our
school which we've built.

00:27:23.597 --> 00:27:25.220
We feed them. We clothe them.

00:27:25.220 --> 00:27:26.978
And his project costs

00:27:28.178 --> 00:27:30.744
millions of dollars every few years.

00:27:32.695 --> 00:27:35.292
Although, he doesn't
take a salary properly.

00:27:37.059 --> 00:27:39.334
But why am I talking about that?

00:27:39.334 --> 00:27:41.076
The idea of grasping to money,

00:27:41.076 --> 00:27:42.670
NYE BAR LEN PA,

00:27:42.670 --> 00:27:46.199
the idea of grasping,
that's not what it means.

00:27:46.199 --> 00:27:49.767
So it doesn't mean if you are wealthy,

00:27:49.767 --> 00:27:51.868
or if you know how to acquire money

00:27:51.868 --> 00:27:54.654
and use money that you're a bad person.

00:27:54.654 --> 00:27:55.863
It doesn't mean that.

00:27:55.863 --> 00:27:57.880
And Buddha never meant that.

00:27:57.880 --> 00:28:00.630
So here's the word of grabbing

00:28:00.630 --> 00:28:03.067
that people misunderstand.

00:28:03.067 --> 00:28:06.700
It says, "Don't grab,"
but it doesn't mean money.

00:28:06.700 --> 00:28:08.081
In fact, it means the opposite.

00:28:08.081 --> 00:28:10.843
If you were grabbing to money,

00:28:10.843 --> 00:28:13.422
if you didn't understand
where money was coming from,

00:28:13.422 --> 00:28:16.108
if you want it a lot more quickly,

00:28:16.108 --> 00:28:17.727
you would have to hurt someone

00:28:17.727 --> 00:28:20.478
or rob somebody to get
it. You see what I mean?

00:28:20.478 --> 00:28:23.396
If you thought money
was not coming from you,

00:28:23.396 --> 00:28:26.622
and you thought money was coming this way,

00:28:26.622 --> 00:28:29.674
then automatically you'd be vulnerable

00:28:29.674 --> 00:28:33.092
to hurting people to get money.

00:28:33.092 --> 00:28:34.143
Because that's the only way

00:28:34.143 --> 00:28:36.344
you can get a lot of money quick

00:28:36.344 --> 00:28:39.420
if you had to support John's project.

00:28:39.420 --> 00:28:44.420
But if you understood that
money is flowing from you,

00:28:45.245 --> 00:28:47.119
like this other green...

00:28:47.119 --> 00:28:48.393
[clanking water bottle]

00:28:48.393 --> 00:28:50.017
If you understood it was flowing from you,

00:28:51.567 --> 00:28:53.281
then you could easily plant money.

00:28:53.281 --> 00:28:55.628
And it would be an interesting test

00:28:55.628 --> 00:28:58.585
to make the largest diamond
company in the world,

00:28:58.585 --> 00:29:00.775
to see if your ideas are correct.

00:29:00.775 --> 00:29:03.382
Be sort of a laboratory, you see?

00:29:03.382 --> 00:29:06.990
So why are we talking about that?

00:29:06.990 --> 00:29:09.777
In this verse Nagarjuna is talking about

00:29:09.777 --> 00:29:11.900
the idea of grabbing.

00:29:11.900 --> 00:29:12.749
NYE BAR LEN PA.

00:29:12.749 --> 00:29:14.192
Upādānaṃ.

00:29:14.192 --> 00:29:16.489
Is the name correct, did you look it up?

00:29:16.489 --> 00:29:17.396
Yes, upādānaṃ.

00:29:17.396 --> 00:29:19.861
So grabbing to things.

00:29:19.861 --> 00:29:22.657
And he says, "You know,
if you really understand

00:29:22.657 --> 00:29:23.968
"that things are flowing from you,

00:29:24.926 --> 00:29:29.193
it's going to protect you
forever from grabbing."

00:29:29.193 --> 00:29:32.077
Then you don't get angry,
you don't get selfish,

00:29:32.077 --> 00:29:35.885
you don't hurt people to
get the things you want.

00:29:35.885 --> 00:29:38.946
You just create them, and they come,

00:29:38.946 --> 00:29:42.330
and you make more money,
and you give it away,

00:29:42.330 --> 00:29:43.595
and you make more.

00:29:45.036 --> 00:29:46.672
And it's fun. It's kind of a fun,

00:29:46.672 --> 00:29:49.526
it's fun to prove day by day

00:29:49.526 --> 00:29:51.357
that these ideas are correct

00:29:51.357 --> 00:29:54.294
with such a normal thing as money.

00:29:54.294 --> 00:29:58.386
So I wanted to talk about...

00:29:59.423 --> 00:30:02.773
He says the people who understand

00:30:02.773 --> 00:30:05.301
the real nature of me and mine,

00:30:05.301 --> 00:30:08.943
they overcome grabbing to things

00:30:08.943 --> 00:30:10.799
in the sense of thinking

00:30:10.799 --> 00:30:13.195
or feeling that money comes from itself.

00:30:13.195 --> 00:30:15.806
Well, that the green bottle
is coming from itself.

00:30:15.806 --> 00:30:18.671
So they overcome grabbing.

00:30:18.671 --> 00:30:20.635
And then Choney Lama, he's kind of cute.

00:30:20.635 --> 00:30:24.967
He says, "Hey, you know,
there's four classic grabbings.

00:30:24.967 --> 00:30:26.236
You want to know what they are?"

00:30:26.236 --> 00:30:28.925
And we're like, "Yes, sure, Choney Lama.

00:30:28.925 --> 00:30:31.465
Tell us what are the
four kinds of grabbing?"

00:30:31.465 --> 00:30:35.949
And I thought it'd be cute
if I gave them to you.

00:30:35.949 --> 00:30:37.620
And what's cool about having a databases

00:30:37.620 --> 00:30:40.072
of 16 million pages -

00:30:40.072 --> 00:30:44.308
which is our 35th anniversary
this month, of inputting -

00:30:44.308 --> 00:30:46.129
you can just ask the computer

00:30:46.129 --> 00:30:47.705
what's the four kinds of grabbing.

00:30:47.705 --> 00:30:52.526
And it tells you in exactly
nine seconds or something.

00:30:52.526 --> 00:30:55.929
I used to flip through
the books page by page.

00:30:55.929 --> 00:31:00.088
That would take about a
week to find this answer.

00:31:00.088 --> 00:31:02.927
Now we can do it in about
less than 10 seconds.

00:31:02.927 --> 00:31:05.295
So we can search 16 million pages

00:31:05.295 --> 00:31:07.756
in less than 10 seconds.

00:31:07.756 --> 00:31:09.407
As long as we stick to the input code

00:31:09.407 --> 00:31:12.856
created by grandpa, otherwise
it will take quite a while.

00:31:12.856 --> 00:31:15.033
Anyway, here we go.

00:31:15.033 --> 00:31:17.224
I thought you'd like to know the four.

00:31:17.224 --> 00:31:18.249
First one,

00:31:18.249 --> 00:31:21.649
YUL YID 'ONG LA LHAG PAR
CHAGS PA'I 'DOD CHAGS DANG PO

00:31:21.649 --> 00:31:23.573
Common grabbing,

00:31:23.573 --> 00:31:27.764
which is the pursuit of desirable objects.

00:31:28.950 --> 00:31:30.572
That's called 'common grabbing.'

00:31:30.572 --> 00:31:33.805
Plain old grabbing. It's the
pursuit of desirable objects.

00:31:33.805 --> 00:31:35.163
Is it wrong?

00:31:37.426 --> 00:31:39.258
Not if you do it the right way.

00:31:39.258 --> 00:31:41.273
Money's helpful.

00:31:41.273 --> 00:31:44.253
He spent $15 million dollars.

00:31:44.253 --> 00:31:48.072
He's expensive. And we wouldn't
have the 16 million pages.

00:31:48.072 --> 00:31:49.822
So money can be used.

00:31:50.872 --> 00:31:52.580
Anyways, but this means

00:31:52.580 --> 00:31:56.318
wrong kind of attachment
to pleasurable objects.

00:31:56.318 --> 00:31:59.790
First kind of grabbing, grabbingness.

00:31:59.790 --> 00:32:03.192
Should we translate it that
way, Tim, grabbingness?

00:32:03.192 --> 00:32:07.512
By the way, Tim, this
explanation comes from your book.

00:32:07.512 --> 00:32:08.928
I just thought you'd like to know.

00:32:08.928 --> 00:32:13.523
So Tim... I chose 10 translators.

00:32:13.523 --> 00:32:16.402
I said if anyone shows that
they're really serious,

00:32:16.402 --> 00:32:18.400
I'll take a new one.

00:32:18.400 --> 00:32:22.805
It took five years, and then
he proved he was serious.

00:32:22.805 --> 00:32:25.420
So about a couple of months ago

00:32:25.420 --> 00:32:27.882
he decided he will translate something

00:32:27.882 --> 00:32:29.379
which is a big commitment for me.

00:32:29.379 --> 00:32:30.974
It's a 10-15 year commitment.

00:32:32.066 --> 00:32:34.950
His book is called "The
Perfection of Wisdom,"

00:32:35.825 --> 00:32:38.835
which is really Middle Way school.

00:32:38.835 --> 00:32:40.385
And this is from your book.

00:32:40.385 --> 00:32:43.682
I just thought you'd like to know.

00:32:43.682 --> 00:32:44.666
Second one:

00:32:44.666 --> 00:32:46.021
LTA NGAN LA LHAG PAR CHAGS PA'I 'DOD CHAGS

00:32:46.021 --> 00:32:51.021
Second kind of grabbing is
grabbing to unhelpful ideas,

00:32:51.309 --> 00:32:54.228
hurting people, ideas that hurt people.

00:32:54.228 --> 00:32:56.910
And I would say one of
the big ones in this world

00:32:56.910 --> 00:33:00.988
is competitiveness between companies,

00:33:00.988 --> 00:33:03.913
between people, between countries,

00:33:05.654 --> 00:33:08.245
inside of companies, between employees.

00:33:09.254 --> 00:33:14.254
And it's this sick idea.
It's a very sick idea

00:33:14.425 --> 00:33:18.592
that I can improve my life
if I make your life worse.

00:33:19.965 --> 00:33:23.609
Or that we should compete for resources.

00:33:23.609 --> 00:33:27.592
And by the way, that's the
way not to get resources.

00:33:27.592 --> 00:33:31.725
The smartest way to not
get the resources you need

00:33:32.613 --> 00:33:37.613
would be to compete with someone
else to get more from them.

00:33:38.429 --> 00:33:40.393
Then that would assure that
you will be short of resources

00:33:40.393 --> 00:33:42.481
later, if these ideas are correct.

00:33:43.739 --> 00:33:46.539
Then the opposite, sharing resources,

00:33:46.539 --> 00:33:50.508
would be the way to increase
the market of resources.

00:33:50.508 --> 00:33:53.317
Those countries which share oil

00:33:54.478 --> 00:33:58.042
and natural gas will have
more oil and natural gas

00:33:59.600 --> 00:34:01.096
discovered in their country.

00:34:01.096 --> 00:34:02.490
And there's a beautiful story about that

00:34:02.490 --> 00:34:07.022
how we discovered
natural gas in shale oil.

00:34:07.022 --> 00:34:08.628
Fracking?

00:34:08.628 --> 00:34:10.194
That's a long story.

00:34:11.360 --> 00:34:12.286
LTA NGAN LA LHAG

00:34:12.286 --> 00:34:14.566
So, attachment to these sick views.

00:34:14.566 --> 00:34:16.328
I would call them sick viewpoints.

00:34:16.328 --> 00:34:18.271
I can get more money.

00:34:18.271 --> 00:34:19.927
Zero sum game.

00:34:19.927 --> 00:34:21.861
I get more pizza.

00:34:21.861 --> 00:34:24.257
There's only eight pieces of pizza.

00:34:24.257 --> 00:34:26.639
And if I don't take one from you,

00:34:26.639 --> 00:34:28.339
there won't be more for me.

00:34:28.339 --> 00:34:32.200
Instead of the idea of
buying a second pizza,

00:34:32.200 --> 00:34:35.532
which is what Buddhism would say.

00:34:35.532 --> 00:34:37.815
If you buy infinite number of pizzas,

00:34:37.815 --> 00:34:41.045
maybe there'll be enough
pieces for everybody.

00:34:41.045 --> 00:34:45.418
Third stupid view is to believe in a self

00:34:45.418 --> 00:34:49.356
or a me, and now you know me means a thing

00:34:49.356 --> 00:34:51.706
which is not coming from you.

00:34:51.706 --> 00:34:53.522
Does the phone have a me?

00:34:55.747 --> 00:34:57.612
Depends on how you look at it.

00:34:57.612 --> 00:35:01.000
If you think the phone didn't
come from sharing phones,

00:35:01.867 --> 00:35:04.980
then you are grabbing
to a me of the phone.

00:35:04.980 --> 00:35:07.700
If you think that you always
get a phone by sharing phones...

00:35:07.700 --> 00:35:11.167
I didn't pay for this
phone. Just keeps coming.

00:35:11.167 --> 00:35:14.917
I didn't pay for the
computer, They just come.

00:35:14.917 --> 00:35:19.020
So if you are generous,
and you help others

00:35:19.020 --> 00:35:22.550
with the right understanding
of me and mine,

00:35:22.550 --> 00:35:24.947
they tend to flow back.

00:35:24.947 --> 00:35:26.858
Well, they inevitably flow back.

00:35:26.858 --> 00:35:28.108
Okay, the fourth one.

00:35:28.108 --> 00:35:30.341
BRTUL ZHUGS NGAN PA LA LHAG PAR CHAGS PA

00:35:30.341 --> 00:35:33.584
This is a special kind of grabbing

00:35:33.584 --> 00:35:38.366
that Buddha was particularly offended by

00:35:38.366 --> 00:35:43.366
which was religious practices
which would hurt people.

00:35:44.746 --> 00:35:46.687
So in his time, for example,

00:35:47.652 --> 00:35:52.052
sacrifice of cows, bulls was common.

00:35:53.064 --> 00:35:54.613
Gandhi-ji talks about it.

00:35:55.705 --> 00:35:58.609
One of the heaviest moments in his life

00:35:58.609 --> 00:36:02.340
was when he observed an
animal sacrificed in Varanasi.

00:36:02.340 --> 00:36:04.798
And it changed his life.

00:36:06.204 --> 00:36:10.904
So this idea of religion being...

00:36:12.425 --> 00:36:15.697
that religious practices that focus on

00:36:15.697 --> 00:36:18.753
hurting other people or
hurting other creatures,

00:36:18.753 --> 00:36:21.676
would be religious, is the
fourth kind of grabbing.

00:36:22.560 --> 00:36:24.105
So those are the four kinds of grabbings.

00:36:25.097 --> 00:36:27.072
We covered Choney Lama.

00:36:27.972 --> 00:36:29.832
I've got something here for you a picture.

00:36:29.832 --> 00:36:32.376
I don't remember the picture.

00:36:32.376 --> 00:36:33.871
So, I don't know, I thought

00:36:33.871 --> 00:36:36.122
this was a cool picture for grabbing.

00:36:38.101 --> 00:36:39.868
And they're grabbing.

00:36:41.362 --> 00:36:43.427
And by the way... Yes, okay.

00:36:43.427 --> 00:36:45.927
Let's go onto the next verse.

00:36:48.472 --> 00:36:50.164
I'll read the verse.

00:36:50.164 --> 00:36:51.809
LAS DANG...

00:36:51.809 --> 00:36:54.850
Christina, you have the verse?

00:36:54.850 --> 00:36:56.697
You want to do the Sanskrit for fun?

00:36:56.697 --> 00:36:58.431
Go slow, go super slow.

00:36:59.387 --> 00:37:01.395
And pronounce it correctly.

00:37:04.003 --> 00:37:04.839
Loud.

00:37:04.839 --> 00:37:07.930
- [Christina] Karmakleśakṣayān

00:37:09.273 --> 00:37:10.106
mokṣaḥ

00:37:11.064 --> 00:37:12.489
- [GMR] h

00:37:12.489 --> 00:37:13.322
- [Christina] Mokṣaḥ?

00:37:13.322 --> 00:37:14.347
- [GMR] Mokṣaḥ

00:37:16.020 --> 00:37:20.861
- [Christina] karmakleśā vikalpataḥ

00:37:20.861 --> 00:37:23.619
- [GMR] h, kalpataḥ

00:37:23.619 --> 00:37:25.852
- [Christina] kalpataḥ

00:37:25.852 --> 00:37:29.352
- [GMR] Yes, if you say "ta
ha", you ruin the meter.

00:37:30.313 --> 00:37:35.313
- [Christina] Te prapañcāt prapañcas tu

00:37:35.521 --> 00:37:39.632
śūnyatāyāṃ nirudhyate

00:37:39.632 --> 00:37:41.223
- [GMR] Nice.

00:37:41.223 --> 00:37:43.859
,LAS DANG NYON MONGS ZAD PAS THAR,

00:37:45.029 --> 00:37:48.384
If you want to achieve nirvana or freedom,

00:37:48.384 --> 00:37:52.392
you're going to have to
stop your negative emotions

00:37:52.392 --> 00:37:55.869
and the harmful actions
that those emotions inspire.

00:37:55.869 --> 00:37:57.464
That's the first line.

00:37:57.464 --> 00:38:00.112
First half of the Sanskrit line.

00:38:00.112 --> 00:38:02.396
,LAS DANG NYON MONGS RNAM RTOG LAS,

00:38:02.396 --> 00:38:05.739
Where does karma, where does it come from?

00:38:05.739 --> 00:38:10.445
Where do negative emotions
and karma come from?

00:38:10.445 --> 00:38:12.720
They come from RNAM RTOG,

00:38:12.720 --> 00:38:13.978
wrong thinking.

00:38:15.085 --> 00:38:19.389
And where does that come from is SPROS PA.

00:38:19.389 --> 00:38:21.906
And SPROS PA is a very difficult word

00:38:22.915 --> 00:38:25.898
that means mental constructs.

00:38:30.785 --> 00:38:33.326
Wrong thinking comes
from mental constructs,

00:38:33.326 --> 00:38:34.997
then leads to negative emotions

00:38:34.997 --> 00:38:38.233
which forces you to hurt people.

00:38:38.233 --> 00:38:39.423
I'll say it again.

00:38:39.423 --> 00:38:42.540
Wrong thinking comes from...

00:38:42.540 --> 00:38:43.540
What did I say?

00:38:43.540 --> 00:38:46.910
Wrong ideas lead to negative emotions,

00:38:46.910 --> 00:38:49.670
which cause you to hurt other people.

00:38:49.670 --> 00:38:53.344
And the root of those is some kind of

00:38:53.344 --> 00:38:56.927
wrong idea of where
things are coming from.

00:38:58.467 --> 00:39:01.002
,STONG PA NYID KYIS 'GAG PAR 'GYUR,

00:39:01.002 --> 00:39:05.661
And all of this chain reaction of trouble

00:39:05.661 --> 00:39:09.195
is stopped when you understand emptiness.

00:39:09.195 --> 00:39:11.271
That's the last line.

00:39:11.271 --> 00:39:14.294
And does it remind you of
anything in the ACI courses?

00:39:15.611 --> 00:39:20.611
[GMR laughing]

00:39:21.290 --> 00:39:24.067
We had a quotation of a commentary

00:39:24.067 --> 00:39:26.363
to a book by Maitreya which is called

00:39:26.363 --> 00:39:28.906
"Uttara Tantra" which is not a tantra.

00:39:28.906 --> 00:39:31.256
It's just a normal book
called "Higher Line",

00:39:32.647 --> 00:39:33.605
"A Higher Line."

00:39:36.621 --> 00:39:40.518
There was a lovely description

00:39:40.518 --> 00:39:44.878
of the chain reaction that
causes the trouble in the world.

00:39:44.878 --> 00:39:46.720
There was a lovely description

00:39:46.720 --> 00:39:51.472
of the domino effect

00:39:51.472 --> 00:39:54.938
that leads from not
understanding emptiness,

00:39:54.938 --> 00:39:57.346
up to war,

00:39:57.346 --> 00:39:59.739
or physical suffering, death in the world.

00:39:59.739 --> 00:40:01.810
And I love that section.

00:40:02.793 --> 00:40:05.868
So I went and got it
because it's based on this.

00:40:07.998 --> 00:40:12.225
Maitreya is about 150
years after Nagarjuna.

00:40:12.225 --> 00:40:15.327
He's talking 150 years after Nagarjuna.

00:40:15.327 --> 00:40:19.678
So I thought we would devolve,

00:40:19.678 --> 00:40:21.295
since we have so much extra time,

00:40:23.487 --> 00:40:28.152
to that explanation by Maitreya.

00:40:28.152 --> 00:40:29.737
Ultimately, by Maitreya.

00:40:29.737 --> 00:40:31.552
Maitreya is who?

00:40:31.552 --> 00:40:32.400
- [Christina] Next Buddha.

00:40:32.400 --> 00:40:33.441
- The next Buddha.

00:40:33.441 --> 00:40:35.501
Next Buddha in this world.

00:40:35.501 --> 00:40:36.334
All right.

00:40:36.334 --> 00:40:39.358
Very, very near, unexpectedly near.

00:40:39.358 --> 00:40:40.191
All right,

00:40:42.582 --> 00:40:44.673
and I went and got them for you.

00:40:45.986 --> 00:40:50.443
Again, this is from your author, Tim,

00:40:50.443 --> 00:40:52.733
which is why I chose him for you,

00:40:52.733 --> 00:40:53.983
because he's so sexy.

00:40:55.158 --> 00:40:59.099
So I'll just read it
and we'll go through it.

00:40:59.099 --> 00:41:00.049
RGYUD BLA MA RTZA 'GREL GYI DGONGS PA

00:41:00.049 --> 00:41:01.984
MTHAR THUG YIN TE,

00:41:01.984 --> 00:41:05.029
Here's the ultimate
intention of the next Buddha.

00:41:05.029 --> 00:41:09.756
BDAG 'DZIN GNYIS KYI BAG CHAGS
LA BRTEN NAS DE GNYIS SKYES,

00:41:09.756 --> 00:41:12.952
The trouble starts when
you enter this life

00:41:12.952 --> 00:41:17.473
with seeds in your mind
from your past life

00:41:17.473 --> 00:41:20.671
which automatically inspire you

00:41:20.671 --> 00:41:24.154
to think that the bottle's
coming from the bottle.

00:41:24.154 --> 00:41:26.283
So, that's not your fault.

00:41:26.283 --> 00:41:28.058
That was Joe. Who's Joe?

00:41:30.252 --> 00:41:32.359
I like to call your previous life 'Joe',

00:41:32.359 --> 00:41:35.304
I don't know what his or her name was.

00:41:35.304 --> 00:41:38.067
But it's a joke. It's an inside joke.

00:41:38.067 --> 00:41:41.604
I asked a good friend of mine
why is he such a good person?

00:41:41.604 --> 00:41:44.013
And he said, "Geshe-la,
it wasn't me, it was Joe.

00:41:44.013 --> 00:41:45.762
It was in my past life."

00:41:47.028 --> 00:41:52.028
So, you're at a disadvantage
when you enter this life.

00:41:52.467 --> 00:41:55.215
Because you enter this
life, according to Buddhism,

00:41:55.215 --> 00:41:58.565
with the seeds in your
mind that automatically

00:41:58.565 --> 00:42:01.294
make you feel instinctively that

00:42:01.294 --> 00:42:03.010
the bottle's coming towards you,

00:42:03.010 --> 00:42:04.637
that it came from a factory,

00:42:04.637 --> 00:42:07.642
and it's not coming from your generosity

00:42:07.642 --> 00:42:10.145
of offering water to other people.

00:42:10.145 --> 00:42:12.059
Which is where it's really coming from.

00:42:12.059 --> 00:42:13.967
But you have to re-educate your mind.

00:42:13.967 --> 00:42:17.090
Your mind assumes because of seeds

00:42:17.090 --> 00:42:20.505
that you carried here from your past life,

00:42:20.505 --> 00:42:23.107
that the bottle is coming from the bottle.

00:42:23.107 --> 00:42:26.928
And if we succeed in this life,

00:42:26.928 --> 00:42:30.083
if we use this life correctly,

00:42:30.083 --> 00:42:31.757
by the end of this life,

00:42:31.757 --> 00:42:33.723
our instincts will be the opposite.

00:42:35.447 --> 00:42:37.004
Wow, I love this Pellegrino.

00:42:37.004 --> 00:42:41.015
I'll have to make sure to give
some water to someone today,

00:42:41.015 --> 00:42:43.132
because I want to keep it running.

00:42:44.088 --> 00:42:46.938
And then that's such a
pleasant way to live,

00:42:48.419 --> 00:42:53.419
that you're thanking the
world for all that you have,

00:42:54.137 --> 00:42:56.443
and you're aware that it
comes from your kindness.

00:42:56.443 --> 00:43:01.061
And then that's the whole
feeling of your life

00:43:01.061 --> 00:43:02.314
that you want to keep it going.

00:43:02.314 --> 00:43:04.801
You want to keep being
kind to other people.

00:43:04.801 --> 00:43:09.646
Anyway, he says, "Unfortunately,
we walk into this life

00:43:09.646 --> 00:43:14.114
"with these negative
seeds from the past life.

00:43:14.114 --> 00:43:15.631
"And that's why

00:43:18.212 --> 00:43:20.574
"automatically, without thinking,

00:43:20.574 --> 00:43:24.355
"since you came out of your mother,

00:43:24.355 --> 00:43:28.981
"even an infant, even an ant,
is automatically thinking,

00:43:28.981 --> 00:43:30.820
"things are coming this way.

00:43:30.820 --> 00:43:33.478
And not left to yourself."

00:43:35.423 --> 00:43:38.318
I like to call it... it's like
the kids I met in Mongolia.

00:43:38.318 --> 00:43:41.178
So, I used to go to Mongolia a
lot. We have a project there.

00:43:41.178 --> 00:43:42.908
I was trying to save 600 thousand books.

00:43:42.908 --> 00:43:44.538
Currently saying 300 thousand books.

00:43:44.538 --> 00:43:46.713
John's going to do that deal.

00:43:49.154 --> 00:43:51.305
One night, coming home from the library,

00:43:52.297 --> 00:43:57.289
I saw a sewer cover lift up,

00:43:58.470 --> 00:44:02.141
and an animal ran past me, like a wolf.

00:44:02.141 --> 00:44:04.280
And I was so freaked out.

00:44:04.280 --> 00:44:06.865
Because there's no electricity enough

00:44:06.865 --> 00:44:08.488
for lights on the streets.

00:44:08.488 --> 00:44:11.197
And I just felt this
animal rushed past me.

00:44:13.111 --> 00:44:16.214
And I asked this friend,
this Mongolian with me,

00:44:16.214 --> 00:44:18.103
I said, "What is that?"

00:44:18.103 --> 00:44:20.112
"What's that animal? What
kind of animal can lift up

00:44:20.112 --> 00:44:23.387
a sewer cover and run down the street?"

00:44:23.387 --> 00:44:24.733
He says, "Geshe-la, that's not animals.

00:44:24.733 --> 00:44:26.550
Those are kids. Feral kids."

00:44:28.258 --> 00:44:29.924
And I'm like, "What?"

00:44:29.924 --> 00:44:31.531
And he says, "Their parents died."

00:44:32.664 --> 00:44:35.017
And I said, "Well, what are
they doing in the sewers?"

00:44:35.017 --> 00:44:36.806
He says, "That's the only
warm place in Mongolia.

00:44:36.806 --> 00:44:40.575
"If you don't have any
money, you live in the sewer.

00:44:40.575 --> 00:44:44.401
"You crawl down in the
sewer and try to stay warm,

00:44:44.401 --> 00:44:46.476
next to the warm water."

00:44:46.476 --> 00:44:47.459
And I'm like, "Uh!"

00:44:47.459 --> 00:44:48.405
And he said,

00:44:48.405 --> 00:44:51.260
"Don't get near them
Geshe-la, they'll bite you.

00:44:51.260 --> 00:44:54.037
"They'll bite you, and
they're very dangerous.

00:44:54.037 --> 00:44:56.480
They'll give you rabies."

00:44:56.480 --> 00:44:58.657
And I'm like, "Whoa!

00:44:58.657 --> 00:44:59.811
Can't you talk to them?"

00:44:59.811 --> 00:45:01.338
He says, "They don't know language.

00:45:01.338 --> 00:45:03.821
"They never learned language.
They didn't have parents.

00:45:03.821 --> 00:45:06.985
They never had parents. They
are feral. They are animals."

00:45:08.918 --> 00:45:10.785
And there's a lady who got a Nobel Prize

00:45:10.785 --> 00:45:12.200
by trying to take care of them.

00:45:13.034 --> 00:45:15.393
We used to help her. But anyway,

00:45:15.393 --> 00:45:16.738
why am I saying that?

00:45:17.804 --> 00:45:21.305
Left to yourself in this lifetime

00:45:21.305 --> 00:45:26.296
without any other
competing thought system,

00:45:27.841 --> 00:45:30.731
you would believe that this
was coming from itself.

00:45:30.731 --> 00:45:33.588
And you would fight to get
them when you wanted them.

00:45:33.588 --> 00:45:36.804
And in the end, if push came to shove...

00:45:36.804 --> 00:45:41.804
I was in Hawaii, surfing,
when the gas thing happened,

00:45:41.813 --> 00:45:43.963
the Oil Embargo happened,

00:45:43.963 --> 00:45:45.690
and people were in line

00:45:45.690 --> 00:45:49.329
to get gas every other
day. And people had guns.

00:45:50.321 --> 00:45:52.719
And people were shooting
each other for gas.

00:45:52.719 --> 00:45:55.448
He says, "You think you're so civilized.

00:45:55.448 --> 00:45:58.447
"But if push came to shove,

00:45:58.447 --> 00:45:59.658
"and it was the last bottle of water,

00:45:59.658 --> 00:46:01.994
"and you didn't understand
that it comes from you,

00:46:01.994 --> 00:46:04.442
you would hurt someone else to get it."

00:46:04.442 --> 00:46:07.350
So we're feral. We're born feral,

00:46:08.251 --> 00:46:09.392
because of our last life.

00:46:09.392 --> 00:46:10.574
We come into this life thinking

00:46:10.574 --> 00:46:13.487
that the water is there, and
that it's not coming from

00:46:13.487 --> 00:46:15.157
how I treat other people.

00:46:15.157 --> 00:46:17.642
We are feral by nature, number one.

00:46:17.642 --> 00:46:22.058
Two. Then you start to believe that

00:46:22.058 --> 00:46:24.887
the thing exists out there.

00:46:24.887 --> 00:46:26.847
Due to the seeds in your last life

00:46:26.847 --> 00:46:29.996
you start to believe that
things exist out there,

00:46:29.996 --> 00:46:32.507
and that they're coming this way.

00:46:32.507 --> 00:46:35.156
Then your seed, what do you call it,

00:46:35.156 --> 00:46:36.696
your selection of seeds in your mind

00:46:36.696 --> 00:46:39.052
makes you perceive some
things as desirable

00:46:39.052 --> 00:46:41.296
and some things as undesirable.

00:46:41.296 --> 00:46:42.773
Like this is nice water.

00:46:42.773 --> 00:46:46.653
I don't personally like LaCroix water.

00:46:46.653 --> 00:46:49.586
So that's also your seeds operating.

00:46:52.987 --> 00:46:55.203
Due to that, comes up...

00:46:57.012 --> 00:46:58.657
This water looks good to me.

00:46:58.657 --> 00:47:01.301
That canned water doesn't look good to me.

00:47:01.301 --> 00:47:03.891
Due to those two misperceptions,

00:47:03.891 --> 00:47:06.437
thinking that the water comes this way,

00:47:06.437 --> 00:47:10.145
I start to want one,
and not want the other.

00:47:10.145 --> 00:47:13.598
That's a natural reaction.
When one water looks nice,

00:47:13.598 --> 00:47:15.285
and the other water doesn't look nice,

00:47:15.285 --> 00:47:18.527
I would rather have the
one that looks nice to me.

00:47:18.527 --> 00:47:21.844
So then you get this thing
called liking and disliking.

00:47:21.844 --> 00:47:22.752
What do we call it?

00:47:22.752 --> 00:47:25.727
Ignorant liking and ignorant disliking.

00:47:25.727 --> 00:47:28.931
And that triggers karma. Why?

00:47:28.931 --> 00:47:31.256
You fight for the things you want.

00:47:31.256 --> 00:47:33.981
You fight to get away from
the things you don't want.

00:47:33.981 --> 00:47:36.464
And that's karma. That creates karma.

00:47:36.464 --> 00:47:40.447
Due to that, you plant
seeds for next time.

00:47:42.248 --> 00:47:44.373
So then it starts to become a cycle.

00:47:44.373 --> 00:47:47.563
So what Nagarjuna's
explaining in this verse

00:47:48.504 --> 00:47:50.928
is that same cycle.

00:47:50.928 --> 00:47:54.453
If you never figure out that
things are coming from you,

00:47:57.148 --> 00:47:58.664
you will repeat.

00:47:58.664 --> 00:47:59.655
What do you call it?

00:47:59.655 --> 00:48:01.754
Reboot. Repeat.

00:48:01.754 --> 00:48:05.541
You'll just keep going like this and this.

00:48:05.541 --> 00:48:08.949
Your life will continue
to be a downward spiral,

00:48:10.028 --> 00:48:15.028
and it will end in the
inevitable death and poverty,

00:48:15.176 --> 00:48:18.046
the ultimate poverty where
you even don't own your body.

00:48:21.050 --> 00:48:26.050
So anyway, he's saying
that's the typical cycle.

00:48:26.154 --> 00:48:27.809
Can we break it?

00:48:27.809 --> 00:48:31.165
Can you break it? That's
coming up in the next class,

00:48:31.165 --> 00:48:32.727
which you have to come to

00:48:32.727 --> 00:48:34.683
if you want to know how to break it.

00:48:35.774 --> 00:48:37.458
Okay, see you then.

